Chapter: RocIterDropIf
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: iterator-like drop_if stops at first kept tag payload". The
 mirror of `roc-iter-keep-if`, and Roc keeps both:

     drop_if : Iter(a), (a -> Bool) -> Iter(a)
     drop_if = |iter, pred|
         { next: || match (iter.next)() {
             One({ item, rest }) =>
                 if pred(item) { (Iter.drop_if(rest, pred).next)() }
                 else { One({ item, rest: Iter.drop_if(rest, pred) }) }
             Done => Done } }
     dropped = Iter.drop_if(range_to(1, 4), |item| item == 1)

 and Roc expects 2.

 The same two adaptations `roc-iter-map.codex` records -- Codex has no
 nullary function type, so the thunk takes an ignored Integer; and Codex
 has no anonymous record, so `One` carries its two as constructor fields.

 **What separates this from `roc-iter-keep-if`, which is the reason the
 suite has both.** The two are the same program with the branches swapped,
 and the swap moves which branch is the recursive one. In keep_if the
 REJECT branch builds a fresh iterator and immediately enters it; here the
 KEEP branch does the rebuilding and the reject branch is the one that
 recurses through a record field of a value nothing bound. So the same
 defect would surface on different data: keep_if needs a rejected element
 to reach it, drop_if needs a kept one. `1` is dropped and `2` is kept, so
 this file exercises both branches once before it answers.

 Neither has run. `roc-iter-keep-if` was committed unexercised and this
 lands the same way.

Section: Types

  Iter (a) = record {
   next : Integer -> Step a
  }

  Step (a) = | One (a) (Iter a) | Done

Section: Functions

  iter-drop-if : Iter a, (a -> Boolean) -> Iter a
  iter-drop-if (it) (pred) =
   Iter { next = \ignored -> when ((it.next) 0)
     is One (item) (rest) -> if pred item then ((iter-drop-if rest pred).next) 0
        else One item (iter-drop-if rest pred)
     is Done -> Done }

  range-to : Integer, Integer -> Iter Integer
  range-to (start) (stop) =
   Iter { next = \ignored -> if start == stop then Done
     else One start (range-to (start + 1) stop) }

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   let dropped = iter-drop-if (range-to 1 4) (\item -> item == 1)
   in when ((dropped.next) 0)
    is One (item) (rest) -> print-line-uni (show item)
    is Done -> print-line-uni (show (0 - 1))
  end
